    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do I reach the starting point?",
        "answer": "Our journey begins and ends in Guwahati. Fly into Lokpriya Gopinath Bordoloi International Airport (GAU) or arrive at Guwahati Railway Station. We coordinate a common pickup point at the airport or Khanapara."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is there a permit required for Meghalaya or Assam?",
        "answer": "For Indian citizens, no Inner Line Permit (ILP) is required to visit Meghalaya or Assam. You just need to carry a valid Government-issued photo ID (Aadhaar, Voter ID, or Driving License)."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the best time for this \"Forests & Falls\" combo?",
        "answer": "November to April is ideal for Kaziranga Safari (park closed May to October) and clear waters in Dawki. September to October is great for chasing massive waterfalls in Meghalaya."
      },
      {
        "question": "How difficult is the Double Decker Root Bridge trek?",
        "answer": "It is a physically demanding trek involving approximately 3,500 steps one way (2–3 hours down, 3–4 hours back up). Basic fitness is recommended."
      },
      {
        "question": "What kind of Safari happens in Kaziranga?",
        "answer": "We offer an early morning Jeep Safari, which is great for covering more ground and spotting One-Horned Rhinos, wild buffaloes, and tigers."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I swim in the waterfalls?",
        "answer": "Yes, in designated spots like Krang Suri (life jackets mandatory). Please dress modestly while walking through the local villages."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should I pack for this trip?",
        "answer": "Meghalaya: Rain poncho, sturdy trekking shoes, quick-dry towel. Kaziranga: Earthy/dull-colored clothes (khaki, olive, brown). General: Power bank, insect repellent, motion sickness meds."
      },
      {
        "question": "Will I have a mobile network?",
        "answer": "Excellent 4G/5G in Shillong & Guwahati. Decent network in Cherrapunji & Kaziranga. Poor or no network in Dawki & Nongriat village."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the food like?",
        "answer": "Expect a mix of Assamese and Khasi cuisine (rice, dal, local vegetables, Jadoh, Masor Tenga). Vegetarian and vegan options are always available."
      },
      {
        "question": "How many people are in a typical group?",
        "answer": "We keep our groups small and intimate, usually between 10 to 16 travelers."
      }
    ],
